% A model with exactly ONE solution, whose three variables take three different values.
%
% Two things the other models cannot catch. First, the CLI has to bridge two variable
% index spaces -- the solver's Var.t and the model's declaration order -- and every other
% model here is either single-variable or symmetric enough that a permutation between
% them would still print something plausible. Here a permuted bridge prints 3, 2, 1.
%
% Second, the expected output does not depend on the search strategy. The solution is
% unique (b = a+1, c = b+1, a+b+c = 6 forces a = 1), so this file states a fact about the
% model rather than a fact about first-fail and indomain_min -- which matters under
% invariant I-M1, where the expected output is ground truth and may not be edited to
% match whatever the solver currently does.
array [1..3] of int: ones = [1, 1, 1];
array [1..2] of int: diff = [1, -1];
var 0..9: a :: output_var;
var 0..9: b :: output_var;
var 0..9: c :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_eq(ones, [a, b, c], 6);
constraint int_lin_eq(diff, [b, a], 1);
constraint int_lin_eq(diff, [c, b], 1);
solve satisfy;
